**Convention 2.2 (supported equality consequences).** JR42, RH Theorem
2.2 and the sharp-cost proof give, for every competitor at sharp cost,
the supported live map $D(\cdot)D^\dagger+T(\cdot)T^\dagger+S(\cdot)S^\dagger$,
total supported failure effect $M$, and no other supported output mass.
The supported high acquired effect is
$(1-u_s)D^\dagger D+(1-v_s)T^\dagger T$. Each high label has no
supported later failure. These are supported consequences for all
optimizers; they do not identify whole five-mode channels or individual
labelled failure-time measures.

On the weak interval every nonzero low encoder/live-decoder product is,
after removal of a common phase,

$$
K(c)=c_0D+c_1T+c_2S,\qquad
c_0^2=\theta c_1^2+\omega c_2^2,\quad c_0\ne0.
\tag{TA.11}
$$

Its normalized coefficient vector is $f=L^{-1}c$, with $f^TQf=0$;
the total normalized low Gram is $I_3$. RH Lemma 8.2 proves one
real-null ray per actual low live-bearing label, including arbitrary
inaccessible multiplicity. RH Theorem 9.5 proves the minimum of such
labels is three through $s_\dagger$, including contact, and four for
$s_\dagger<s\leq s_0$. This is credited low-label mathematics, not
the total-alphabet theorem below.

For strong $s>s_0$, JR's equality conditions give four low coefficient
rays and two high directions,

$$
\begin{aligned}
K_{\sigma,\eta}&=
 (\sqrt{u_s}D+\sigma\sqrt{v_s}T+\eta S)/2,\\
H_\sigma&=
 (\sqrt{1-u_s}D+\sigma\sqrt{1-v_s}T)/\sqrt2,
\qquad\sigma,\eta=\pm1.
\end{aligned}
\tag{TA.12}
$$

All four low ray masses are positive, their rank is two, and both high
matrices are invertible. The low height differences for $(\sigma,\eta)$
equal $(+,+),(+,-),(-,+)$ are strictly positive. Only $(-,-)$ can be
negative or zero. The supported high Gram is
$\operatorname{diag}(1-u_s,1-v_s,0)$, with zero $DT$ cross moment.
These are JR's real equality face and strong height result; the actual
high-label count will require a separate report argument.

For later use set

$$
\begin{gathered}
\Xi=M^{1/2}\Delta M^{1/2}\big|_{\{1,2\}}
 =\begin{pmatrix}-d_f&z_f\\z_f&0\end{pmatrix},\quad
d_f=11m_1/64>0,\quad
z_f=\frac{\sqrt{m_1m_2}}{16}(\sqrt{37}-\sqrt{59})<0,\\
L_f=\sqrt{d_f^2+4z_f^2},\qquad T_{\rm flat}=(d_f+L_f)/2.
\end{gathered}
\tag{TA.13}
$$

TP implies $\Delta A+\Delta B+\Delta C=d_f$ and
$(1-\delta)\Delta A+\operatorname{tr}G=d_f$. JR's exact weak
reduction is

$$
\mathcal T(s_0\tau)=\frac{(1-\delta)\Delta A+L_f+J(\tau)}2,
\tag{TA.14}
$$

where $J$ maximizes $\sum_{r,\epsilon}\epsilon\operatorname{tr}(GX_{r,\epsilon})$
over real PSD slots with individual zero $Q$ trace, total $I_3$ and
$\sum_{r,\epsilon}r(X_{r,\epsilon})_{01}\geq\tau$.
Analytic separation/refinement upper-bounds an arbitrary actual record's
TV; a new compatible instrument actually generating the labels attains
the bound. It does not expose old inaccessible indices.

